1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,920
 In this lecture, we're going to look at a few more AWS services

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:08,360
 in the area of security, governance, and compliance.

3
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:11,520
 So the first one is Amazon Inspector.

4
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:14,120
 So Amazon Inspector automatically

5
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:19,160
 discovers compute workload, such as Amazon EC2, instances,

6
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:24,560
 container images in ECR and within CACD and Lambda function.

7
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:30,520
 Now, what does the Amazon Inspector do?

8
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:34,560
 It scans for software vulnerabilities

9
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:38,920
 and unintended network exposure.

10
00:00:38,920 --> 00:00:41,600
 In the exam, you need to keep in mind two things

11
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:42,960
 about Amazon Inspector.

12
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:46,280
 One, it is for compute workloads.

13
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:49,800
 Second, it scans for software vulnerabilities

14
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:54,280
 and unintended network exposure.

15
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:57,160
 OK, just let me just show you through the console quick.

16
00:00:57,160 --> 00:00:59,920
 So if I just go and search for Inspector.

17
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:08,600
 So right now, you can see I am not enabled Amazon Inspector.

18
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:13,440
 So we need to activate the Inspector in order for it to work.

19
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:14,960
 And there's going to be some charge for it.

20
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:16,520
 So I'm not going to activate Inspector.

21
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:18,520
 But you can see here Amazon Inspector

22
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:20,520
 is an automated vulnerability management

23
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:24,520
 service that consistently scans workloads for vulnerabilities

24
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:27,840
 and unintended network exposure.

25
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:30,000
 So if you activate Inspector, then it's

26
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,520
 going to scan all your compute workloads

27
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:37,440
 and then generate a report for you.

28
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:40,800
 Now, let's take a look at the second service,

29
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:44,240
 which is AWS trusted advisor.

30
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:48,120
 So AWS trusted advisor continuously

31
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:53,760
 evaluates your AWS environment using best practices

32
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:57,040
 along multiple categories.

33
00:01:57,040 --> 00:01:59,640
 So those categories are cost optimization

34
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:01,240
 and security.

35
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:05,080
 Then you have resilience, performance, operational excellence.

36
00:02:05,080 --> 00:02:07,240
 Then you have another really important one, which

37
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:09,480
 is called as service limits.

38
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:12,400
 So what AWS trusted advisor does is it

39
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:17,240
 will review how you are using all the AWS services

40
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:22,600
 within your account across these six different categories

41
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:26,880
 and then compare them with the AWS best practices.

42
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:31,160
 And then it would recommend actions to remediate

43
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:34,440
 any deviations from best practices.

44
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:37,880
 Now, let's take a look at all these six different categories

45
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:38,880
 from the console.

46
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:40,520
 So I am on the AWS console.

47
00:02:40,520 --> 00:02:42,800
 Let me just search for trusted advisor.

48
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:48,560
 Now on the left, you can see recommendations.

49
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:50,560
 And here are the six categories.

50
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:53,000
 So if I just click on cost optimization.

51
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,640
 So here it defines what it means by cost optimization.

52
00:02:57,640 --> 00:03:00,240
 Choose a check name to see recommendations

53
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:04,240
 for a ways to help save money for your AWS account.

54
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:08,080
 So now these are the various best practices

55
00:03:08,080 --> 00:03:13,000
 that it would check the resource that you are using

56
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,280
 and see if it is cost optimized.

57
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:18,480
 So in my case, I don't have an ECR repository.

58
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:21,120
 If I had configured one, then it would check

59
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:26,120
 whether I have Amazon ECR repository without a life cycle

60
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:29,640
 policy, then it will check whether the AWS account is part

61
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:34,640
 of AWS organization or not, whether my EBS volumes

62
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:36,640
 are overprovision.

63
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:40,200
 So if they are overprovision, it would recommend

64
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:43,120
 to reduce the size of the EBS volume.

65
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:44,840
 The second one is the performance.

66
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:47,760
 And here it will recommend how to improve the performance

67
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:49,480
 of AWS services.

68
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:52,480
 It will check if Amazon DynamoDB auto scaling

69
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:53,720
 has been enabled.

70
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:57,000
 It will check Amazon EBS is underprovision.

71
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,240
 So it will look at all the AWS services

72
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:02,920
 that I have provision and compare it with the best practices

73
00:04:02,920 --> 00:04:04,680
 and then give me some recommendations.

74
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:08,400
 So I don't have any of these services provision right now.

75
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:11,000
 So that's why it's graying out these areas.

76
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,200
 But this is what is gonna check this.

77
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:15,840
 So similarly for security, it will give me suggestions

78
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:19,400
 on how to improve the security posture for my infrastructure.

79
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:23,280
 It will check if there are any Amazon EBS public snapshots,

80
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:27,080
 Amazon RDS public snapshots, obviously you should not have

81
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:30,280
 public snapshots of your database.

82
00:04:30,280 --> 00:04:32,160
 Then it will look at fault tolerance

83
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:36,040
 and here it will suggest ways to increase their availability

84
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:39,440
 and redundancy of my services.

85
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:42,680
 So it will take a look at Amazon DynamoDB

86
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:46,440
 point in time recovery, Amazon Redshift cluster,

87
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:49,560
 automated snapshots and a lot of other things.

88
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:52,400
 Then we have the service limits.

89
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:56,040
 I think this is the most important use case

90
00:04:56,040 --> 00:05:01,040
 for trusted advisor where it would give an alert

91
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:05,640
 if any of the services reach 80% of the service quota.

92
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:09,440
 So what it means is like if you see for each AWS service,

93
00:05:09,440 --> 00:05:13,120
 let's take the example of revenue function in most accounts

94
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:18,120
 and regions, you have the limit of about 1000 invocations.

95
00:05:18,120 --> 00:05:23,200
 And if the invocations hit more than 1000,

96
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:25,320
 then there's going to be throttling.

97
00:05:25,320 --> 00:05:27,000
 So if you're expecting that the invocations

98
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
 are going to be more than 1000.

99
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,800
 So what you can do is you can go to this link service quota

100
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:36,120
 or raise a ticket for AWS support center,

101
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:40,080
 where you can request for additional invocations.

102
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:43,080
 So what AWS does is let's say for lambda function

103
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:46,480
 and let's say the quota limit is 1000.

104
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:49,200
 So when you reach 80% of the limit,

105
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:51,600
 it will send you an yellow alert.

106
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:55,560
 If you reach 100% then it will send you a red alert.

107
00:05:55,560 --> 00:05:59,520
 Okay, then finally you have the operational excellence

108
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:03,480
 where it helps you improve the operational readiness

109
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:05,400
 of your resources.

110
00:06:06,160 --> 00:06:09,240
 And these are some of the things from operational perspective

111
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:12,840
 that it will evaluate against the AWS best practice.

112
00:06:12,840 --> 00:06:17,360
 So Amazon, VPC without flow logs and many others.

113
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:18,640
 So from the exam perspective,

114
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:21,080
 what you need to remember is that there is a service called

115
00:06:21,080 --> 00:06:26,080
 as trusted advisor, which will evaluate all the resources

116
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:30,200
 within your AWS account and map them

117
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:35,080
 to the AWS best practices along these six different areas.

118
00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:37,520
 And if there is a deviation,

119
00:06:37,520 --> 00:06:41,960
 then it is good to provide recommendations and alert you.

120
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:44,680
 Okay, that's pretty much it from this lecture.

121
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:46,120
 I'll see in the next lecture. Thank you.

